    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We studied the oxygen and carbon isotope values of calcite from fault rock and fragmented platform carbonate samples collected along large, basin bounding normal fault zones in central Italy. After analyzing the following elements: (i) cement, (ii) vein, (iii) coated slickenside, (iv) red staining present along fracture walls, (v) platform carbonate host rock, (vi) survivor clast, (vii) carbonate matrix, (viii) uncoated slickensides, and (ix) residual material of pressure solution seams, we were able to characterize two different sources of the fault fluid and their mineralization temperature. The two trends in 𝛿18O- 𝛿13C space are consistent with the flow of infiltrated meteoric water and CO2-enriched groundwater through the normal faults. The internal architecture of the normal faults is made up of faulted and brecciated Quaternary sediments in the hanging wall, major slip surfaces, and matrix/cement-supported fault rocks, pulverized carbonates, and fragmented carbonates in the footwall. The cemented fault rocks mainly localize along the major slip surfaces. The stable isotope composition of samples collected from different structural domains shows fluid focus and compartmentalization primarily at the hanging wall/footwall contact of the faults. Considering the groundwater source of the fault fluid, pockets of high-pressure, CO2-rich fluids may have formed in the upper levels of the faults. There is no isotopic evidence of deep-seated fluids mineralizing within the normal faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
